Title: The Quiet Part
Author: Plotkraft Demo

INT. NEWSROOM - NIGHT

Empty desks. MAYA ORTEGA, 30s, reads the same paragraph for the fourth time. On her screen: a leaked personnel file. One name repeats. KESSLER.

MAYA
(under her breath)
You signed it. You actually signed it.

She copies the file to a drive.

INT. EDITOR'S OFFICE - MORNING

REEVES, 60s, does not touch the drive Maya has placed on his desk.

MAYA
Three sources. The signature is his.

REEVES
We're not running it.

MAYA
You haven't read it.

REEVES
I don't need to.

EXT. FIRE ESCAPE - MORNING

Maya, phone to her ear, watches traffic crawl below.

MAYA
He killed it. So we publish it ourselves.

NOAH (V.O.)
That ends your career, you understand that.

MAYA
It ends his.

INT. NEWSROOM - LATE AFTERNOON

Maya works a legal pad. The paper's lawyer, GIBBS, leans in the doorway, unimpressed.

GIBBS
If he's a public figure, you need actual malice. Knowledge of falsity, or reckless disregard.

MAYA
And if he isn't?

GIBBS
Then negligence gets you sued anyway. The retraction window in this state is ten days from written demand.

MAYA
I filed a records request in March. Twenty business days, they said.

GIBBS
Federal FOIA is twenty. State is ten. You've been reading the wrong statute.

INT. PARKING GARAGE - NIGHT

Maya waits by a concrete pillar, phone face-up on the roof of her car. The source agreed to nine o'clock. It is nine.

MAYA
(to herself)
Come on. You called me.

INT. PARKING GARAGE - LATER

The garage is empty. Maya's phone shows one message: a deleted account. Her only corroborating witness is gone.

MAYA
(into the phone)
Noah. We're on our own.

INT. NOAH'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

NOAH, 40s, builds a bare-bones site while Maya reads pages aloud. Coffee cups multiply.

NOAH
Once this is live, there's no version where you keep your job.

MAYA
Publish it.

INT. DINER - DAY

Maya's sister ANNA pushes a plate of fries toward her. Neither of them mentions the story.

ANNA
Mom asked about you again.

MAYA
Tell her I'm fine.

ANNA
I've been telling her that for six years.

INT. NOAH'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Noah hits publish. The page goes live. For a moment neither of them says anything, and it feels like winning.

MAYA
That's it. It's out.

INT. NOAH'S APARTMENT - LATER THAT NIGHT

A letter from Kessler's counsel lands in the inbox. Noah reads it twice and says nothing.

NOAH
They responded in fifty minutes. On a Sunday.

INT. NOAH'S APARTMENT - LATER

Noah has the original file open beside a scan of the real personnel record. The signature does not match.

NOAH
Maya. The file's a forgery.

MAYA
That's not possible, I verified —

NOAH
You verified the copy. Somebody wanted this published.

INT. NEWSROOM - DAY

Maya crosses the floor to Reeves' office. Every screen behind her carries her name, and not kindly.

INT. EDITOR'S OFFICE - CONTINUOUS

Reeves finally picks up the drive.

REEVES
I knew it was bait the moment it landed.

MAYA
Then why not tell me?

REEVES
Because you'd have published it anyway. You needed to be wrong about something first.
